{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/1054723-how-do-you-run-exalted-solars-game-without-going-down-the-meta-plot-road","id":1054723,"title":"How do you run Exalted/Solars game without going down the meta-plot road?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted","Solar Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-02-25T05:52:51-08:00","id":1054723,"title":"How do you run Exalted/Solars game without going down the meta-plot road?","content":"I've run a handful of long term games with solars that have gone beyond a dozen sessions. In each instance, despite different settings and conceptual set ups for each game, the underlying meta-plot always creeps in - the Solar's begin to take charge of increasingly larger areas/people and fight back against one of the big bads of the game. Despite differences, all games end up feeling a bit... samey. <br /> <br /> Unless you start the game with the solars already in charge of a city/state/nation, the rise-to-power narrative seems destined to always dominate the first stage of any campaign. Has anyone found ways to avoid this meta-plot taking control of your game?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Visit me at <a href=\"http://www.bragrman.com\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Tales of Grey</i></a> - my RPG Game-Master's blog.<br /> <br /> &quot;If only I had an enemy bigger than my apathy, I could have won&quot; - <i>I gave you all,</i> Mumford &amp; Sons","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8520-capitantypo","name":"CapitanTypo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8520_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"896"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T06:08:43-08:00","id":1054727,"title":"","content":"That's not really what a metaplot is, in either the normal sense <i>or</i> the way in which it has sprung up in RPG parlance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 02-25-2017, 09:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T07:42:09-08:00","id":1054747,"title":"","content":"It's not, but it's still a good question, because I have found it a bit too. Exalted is big enough that each game should feel completely different, but it hasn't really. Having run it for a few years I got bored of it; 3rd ed reinspired me, but then I got bored of it again. It does get a bit samey.<br /> <br /> I guess the question is... what else do you want to do with Solars?<br /> <br /> I mean, I've run essentially two games (the same canon, but in 2nd and 3rd ed, with some different characters and a big time-gap between, and I think the character of the game was quite different).<br /> <br /> In one game, the PCs did fight various Moderate Bads and eventually a Big Bad (a Deathlord). But they did not rise to power over a bigger and bigger region, or really, any region. They sometimes put friendly rulers in charge of places, but then tended to move on.<br /> <br /> In the 3rd ed, I was tired of big bads and generic meta-plot, so there was no Deathlords, Yozis, and the Realm didn't really get involved in the region until right at the end of the game (and then not directly, they tricked the PCs into destroying one of their enemies). But I would say the PCs did rise to dominate an area (mostly not directly, but through puppet kings and terror; though one PC did create and rule a magical mountain kingdom). Not a very big one though. They just weren't interested in direct rule; they were interested in protecting a certain area from their foes, and so hegemony of buffer states was the easiest way to do that.<br /> <br /> Really, I think it depends on the PCs. Maybe they're not interested in rulership.<br /> Avoiding yet another Big Bad fight against the Deathlords or Realm on the other hand, really depends on the ST. Put the game somewhere far away from the normal bad guys (the new Dreaming Sea region is good for this I think, but so is the far north-east, south-west, etc). Just don't run plots related to the normal Big Bads.<br /> <br /> For example, in my recent 3rd ed game, there were essentially 5 main antagonistical groups: the Realm, the Leopard Empire (Harborhead/Varang and some other areas, ruled by a God-Blooded daughter of Ahlat), the Ascended (a cabal of sorcerers), the Sons of Shadow (a demon cult run by Lunars) and the Crimson Masters (a circle of Solars with their own religion aiming to conquer heaven and free humanity from the tyranny of the gods; the Solars also led a barbarian horde). Of these, the Realm were the ones who came up the least, as they were careful not to get into direct conflict with the PCs, but rather use puppets. They'd conquered a city before the game, overextending themselves in the region, so were just consolidating to possibly become a major force in the region later if the Dragonblood book inspired me and the players (but it still hasn't come out).<br /> The Deathlords? Nah, they just never came up. Yozis and Infernals? Again, they just never came up. If you don't want to use them, and are careful, you can avoid the PCs getting drawn into fighting them. But admittedly, it's not always easy; especially if the players are familiar with the setting!<br /> I actually promised the players that there would be no fights with Abyssals and no Deathlord plot before the game, as I was fed up with it.<br /> Instead, I made up my own villains and used those, and just had the normal Big Bads not come up. The first plot had the PCs fight the aforementioned cabal of sorcerers, and later the PCs drew themselves into conflict with the rival Solar circle (which was convenient for me, as the Core has plenty of options for Solars, unlike Sidereals or Lunars!).<br /> I also had a giant marauding behemoth, but the PCs avoided it.<br /> Essentially, I just wanted to have different villains from normal.<br /> <br /> <br /> But, if you want to do a game that doesn't involve the Solars taking over areas and fighting powerful villains <i>at all</i>... I'm not sure what you're going to do instead. That's kind of what Solars do. If you want a game of political intrigue for example, I think Dragonblood work better for that.<br /> <br /> <br /> Saying that, I do also play a very, very long-running game where the PCs have never really taken over anywhere until very, very recently (and not like a whole country, just a small prefecture and an abandoned city in the desert), and have never even seen the Big Bads (most of the party have never heard of Deathlords, and we haven't encountered the Realm in about 5 years RL time). Which is partly because our ST deliberately avoids that kind of thing (we're in the far, far south-east and have been for about 4 or 5 years I think), and partly because several of the main characters avoid that kind of thing. But it's a very weird game in many ways, there's a lot of ridiculous comedy bickering and several of the characters are not very pro-active. The two plotlines I enjoyed most actually did have some big villains (though not the standard Exalted ones; one was my previous PC turned evil, the other was an evil Eunuch with an artefact that allowed him to control ancient Dragon Kings).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 02-25-2017, 10:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T07:45:08-08:00","id":1054748,"title":"","content":"If I ran Exalted again, I would definitely put the game far, far, far away from the standard parts of the setting. And I'd prefer to do a non-Solar game (I think I'd most like to do a Lunar game, but Outcastes might work too). Because they do get a bit samey. So I've stopped running Exalted now, and I run a sci-fi game instead. But then, so do all roleplaying games I guess, when you've played them for nearly a decade. I stopped playing DnD for years and only recently started again. It's just inevitable. But I hope my previous post gives some advice on how to minimise the problem.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T20:16:37-08:00","id":1055059,"title":"","content":"I wish I had that problem. My players tend to run from plot at the first sign of opposition, have no interest in leading anything, and mostly just want to wander around and kill random monsters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/746-kunoichi","name":"Kunoichi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar746_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T20:28:45-08:00","id":1055061,"title":"","content":"That isn't metaplot, that is just setting. Solars are guided by their ambitions, so unless you set the stage for obstacles they are going just Rofflestomp especially since they are in a Circle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T20:30:39-08:00","id":1055062,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kunoichi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/1054723-how-do-you-run-exalted-solars-game-without-going-down-the-meta-plot-road?p=1055059#post1055059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wish I had that problem. My players tend to run from plot at the first sign of opposition, have no interest in leading anything, and mostly just want to wander around and kill random monsters.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah I know mine are like that. They just walk around with their mouths agape and drown in the rain... occasionally killing a giant monster in the process. :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-25T21:46:28-08:00","id":1055082,"title":"","content":"I've actually run and been in a ton of games where the players have just decided they didn't want to be kings, and wanted to stay heroes without ruling. It's easier, more free and more fun a lot of the time, so screw it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-26T01:32:06-08:00","id":1055133,"title":"","content":"Yeah, that's what mine mostly do. They just terrorise rulers into doing what they want, then go home, then come back later to check the rulers are doing what they want. And if the rulers don't, they get replaced by someone who will do what the PCs want.<br /> This does not lead to monarchs with much authority, but it hasn't been a massive issue so far (I think partly because the way monarchy and heroes work is that politics appears much more centralised than it is; if the King is kinging it up in his palace but has little control over the provinces, this won't necessarily be apparent to the PCs).<br /> There is one PC who actually does like ruling stuff, but the others don't care.<br /> <br /> Actually ruling would be too much work.<br /> <br /> In the long-running game I play, after about 8 years real-time since the game began, the Night Caste's wife has eventually got him promoted to Governor/minor Prince, but he didn't really want to do it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-26T02:03:34-08:00","id":1055139,"title":"","content":"My players have a habit of always wanting to waltz right up to the main honcho of a given issue and lay down their Solar proclamations of them, so it's difficult to me to introduce a distant villain with long reach (like say, a deathlord acting through proxies) without accidently escalating the game several layers as they charge into the underworld and slap everyone with golden decrees and obliterate all competition. <br /> <br /> I've just recently introduced a new Solar that is like the Anti-group whose goal is kicking down their sand castles in service to his Sidereal masters, in part to highlight how fragile their hold on nations truly are (if you bullied your way into power then all it takes is a bigger bully to oust you) which has worked wonderfully to get them to buckle down and consolidate now that they know they are not unopposed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(10chars)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2721-elkovash","name":"Elkovash","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2721_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-26T02:46:23-08:00","id":1055147,"title":"","content":"That's a good idea.<br /> <br /> For doing a distant villain with reach, my main strategy was to have the bad guy work through proxies who only knew a piece of the puzzle, so the players had to investigate through a few different people before finding out who the big bad was that they were supposed to punch to death.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-26T12:13:27-08:00","id":1055341,"title":"","content":"I suspect part of it is the path of least resistance. You kind of need an epic quest from the beginning to avoid it otherwise.<br /> <br /> Of the two EX3 games I've been in, one subverted the &quot;rulership&quot; from the start, and the other ended up that way by accident.<br /> <br /> My Night Caste was essentially a bastard daughter from her homeland's king, and her legitimate half-sister is the ACTUAL queen now, so there wasn't much need to 'conquer/acquire your own kingdom' there. She was mostly motivated by protecting what her family already ruled. We ended up peaking the first chronicle, as it were, by talking down a Balorian Crusade level Raksha Queen into peacefully interacting with Creation in return for unsealing her from her prison and her help in stabilizing her fae-blooded amnesiac offspring that was starting to come into her powers.<br /> <br /> My Twilight caste is currently prince-consort because he started working for the royal family of the local city state when a 2nd Circle demon basically showed up on Calibration and said that the next calibration, his 3rd circle master was coming through to hunt the Solar Circle down, and they had a year to prepare. Working with the local royalty was kind of the most logical thing (particularly in avoiding being Wyld Hunted down), and then being the terribly habitual flirt he was, he ended up engaged to the local princess, which TECHNICALLY makes him on the path to ruling the city...but mostly he's her muscle while she handles the politics and he gives her shoulder rubs after court's over for the night. Not exactly super ambitious.<br /> <br /> Technically, the 3rd circle demon could be considered confronting one of the Big Bads, but the Deathlords and Yozis themselves haven't come up yet. The biggest antagonists have been rival city-states and one warlord that turned another of the Circle's girlfriends into a jade statue to fight for him. Taking him down and then arranging a Working to cure her were one of the bigger sidequests.<br /> <br /> However, if you're reductive enough, ANY game is going to lead to &quot;get more powerful, get more influence, fight Big Bad.&quot; Immaculate doctrine makes it hard for Solars to be Solar-y without kind of finding some place (or making one) that accepts them for what they are. However, if you're more roving do-gooders or have some kind of Quest (the Holy Grail, Journey to the West, Cyberutopia, whatever), it's possible to avoid the rulership aspect at least.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1817-opmegs","name":"OpMegs","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1817_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-27T03:00:39-08:00","id":1055579,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I think quests work well. I started off my current game with a quest that most of the PCs were invested in (the traditional rescue-a-kidnapped-friend) and that kept them going for a while before they started settling down to build power bases. But the quest wasn't long enough (it was originally going to be much longer, but I thought I/the players would get bored if it was too long), if you want the game to be about that, it has to be quite long.<br /> <br /> Actually, the first long Solar game I played had a lot of quests which worked quite well, we travelled all over Creation, and it was only when the ST changed and we focused on one small area instead that we began to build influence and a power base (though not much, since the PCs aren't that interested in it).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-28T05:49:39-08:00","id":1056075,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CapitanTypo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/1054723-how-do-you-run-exalted-solars-game-without-going-down-the-meta-plot-road#post1054723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've run a handful of long term games with solars that have gone beyond a dozen sessions. In each instance, despite different settings and conceptual set ups for each game, the underlying meta-plot always creeps in - the Solar's begin to take charge of increasingly larger areas/people and fight back against one of the big bads of the game. Despite differences, all games end up feeling a bit... samey.<br /> <br /> Unless you start the game with the solars already in charge of a city/state/nation, the rise-to-power narrative seems destined to always dominate the first stage of any campaign. Has anyone found ways to avoid this meta-plot taking control of your game?</div> </div> </div> </div>Our group has generally varied the different exalted campaigns by having them stay regional... with a handful being very national &amp; travel-centric... and a rare few being set in different eras. The makeup of the PCs also tends to determine whether its a conquest, protection, hero, or 'other' campaign. I find that shifting the standard agendas of various 'big bads' can help combat the sameyness too. Rather than try to shove every powerful figure into the mix, our games tend to cherry pick a few and/or add new ones. Custom cities &amp; cultures also help to make things a bit newer. We will also tweak/alter timelines as needed to introduce events, write out various npcs, change parts of the setting, etc.<br /> <br /> Since you are aware of a sameyness problem, you're well positioned to tackle it <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I would advise against providing large enemies that would draw the PCs into more 'usual' conflicts (ie Realm families, Deathlords, etc). Maybe look to the Lunar or other supernaturals as a source of conflict instead","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Long time lurker","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8781-happy-hermit","name":"Happy Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8781_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"77"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}